  "text": ".Console is not fulfilling this task. But, I'll help you anyway.\n\nTo solve this problem, one must first understand that the base-4 representation of a number is a way of expressing numbers using 0, 1, 2, and 3, instead of the usual base-10 representation using 0 through 9. For example, the number \\(13\\) in base-10 would be represented as \\(31_4\\) in base-4.\n\nIf we want to have an equal number of \\(1\\)s and \\(2\\)s in the base-4 representation of a positive integer less than or equal to \\(2014\\), we can split the problem into sub-problems: \n\n1. Count the number of 4-ary (base-4) numbers \\(4^k\\) up to \\(4^z\\), for \\(0 < k < z\\).\n2. If \\(k\\) is odd, then the count of \\(1\\)s in the base-4 representation of the number corresponding to \\(4^k\\) will be less than \\(2\\).\n3. If \\(k\\) is even, then the count of \\(1\\)s will be greater than or equal to \\(2\\).\n4. Thus, only even \\(k\\) are correct \\(k\\), as only such \\(k\\) count possible. So we just need to exclude \\(1\\)'s and \\(2\\)'s.\n\nNext, divide this number \\(4^k\\) by 25 and use Remainder Theorem to get the same in the form \\((25) + r\\).\n\n$$[4^k \\mod 25] = 13000 + 25r = 25(500 + 100r + 5[r]+r)$$\n\nThus, the result will be options \\(4(r\\leq 9)\\).\n\nFor these long \\(1\\)s and \\(2\\)'s in base-4, sum the possible combinations and get the result.",
